1. What is the kingdom of God? Provide me with the verses in the NLT.

1. What is the kingdom of darkness? Provide a comparison between the kingdom of darkness and the Kingdom of God, along with relevant verses.

1. Repentance and the Kingdom of God

1. The Kingdom of God is not an earthly kingdom; it is in the hearts of men when the will of the Father is obeyed. Provide relevant verses.

1. The Kingdom of God and His will (with verses)

1. The Kingdom of God is where the will of God as King is obeyed and fulfilled.

1. Repentance, belief, and faith in Christ mean trusting and obeying Him for our salvation. Believing in Him means putting our faith in Christ and His work so that we can go to the Father and enter the Kingdom of God. Does the Father give the kingdom to His Son, or do they share it?

1. Jesus' teachings and discipleship involve becoming like Christ, the perfect image of God. As children of God, we must become like His Son and enter the Kingdom of God.

1. Discipleship, the Great Commission, and the Kingdom of God

1. Discipleship is learning the teachings of Christ and following them to be like Him, since whatever He taught He practiced, and whatever He said and did was according to the will of the Father.

1. Therefore, discipleship is about becoming like Jesus, His Son. The moment we believe in Him and our hearts are regenerated by the Holy Spirit, we become children of God—a right that Christ gives us. As children of God, we must become like our Father by following His Son, who always knows and does His will.

1. Since the Son is the perfect image of the unseen God, if we see and know His Son, we already know His Father. When we are with the Son, we are also with the Father. The Son is the only way to the Father.

1. As children of God, we are led by the Spirit, since we are born again in spirit by the Spirit; indeed, whoever is led by the Spirit is a child of God. No one can become a child of God without the Spirit, and being led by the Spirit means doing the will of God, since it is the Spirit who reminds us of what Jesus taught and enables us to obey things that are according to the will of the Lord.\
   Jesus taught us to obey the will of the Father, and merely knowing the will of the Father is not enough to become a child of God. As children of God, you must do the will of the Lord to enter the Kingdom of God. You can only enter the Kingdom of God if you are born again into His family, becoming an adopted child of God through spiritual rebirth and a heart renewed by the Spirit. It is the Spirit who gives us a new heart to desire the will of the Father and enables us to do so, wrestling with and defeating our sinful nature—our old self and flesh—by walking in the Spirit, letting Him lead us, and allowing Him to transform our minds.

1. The church's mission is the Great Commission, while simultaneously being a bride faithfully waiting for the coming of the Bridegroom, Christ. Christ presents His church without blemish, which means continuous sanctification from sin and the transformation of our hearts to be like Christ, who always knows and does the Father's will.

1. Since the role of the church is to be God's partner in proclaiming and expanding the Kingdom of God through sharing the gospel—preaching repentance and faith in Christ—we work to snatch people from the domain of darkness and the power of sin into the Kingdom of God. In doing so, we are no longer spiritually blind, as Christ shines His light into the domain of darkness. To always remain in His Kingdom, where God's will perfectly reigns, we must continually be transformed to do His will, allowing Him to rule as King in our hearts and letting His Kingdom come within us.

1. Since the Church is not only responsible for spreading the gospel and proclaiming the Kingdom of God—which is evangelism—but also for teaching people how to live in the Kingdom of God by seeking it and doing His will as citizens of heaven—which is discipleship. Therefore, seeking His Kingdom means seeking His will according to His Word, as the Holy Spirit guides us to understand it.

1. Give me verses on being a citizen of heaven. Are the Kingdom of God and the Kingdom of Heaven the same

1. Many people say that heaven is the place you go after you die, but the Bible says that the Kingdom of God is in our hearts if we repent and believe in Christ. Am I correct or not?

1. You mean that we experience the Kingdom of God the moment we repent and believe in Christ, both now and forever, because His Kingdom will endure forever, right?

1. "Therefore, the Church consists of believers who are being discipled in the teachings of Christ to follow the will of the Father. We have already entered the Kingdom of God through the Holy Spirit, and this will extend to the coming of the Bridegroom, Christ, who will reign as King forever. Because His Kingdom endures forever, the Church—meaning us—will live eternally and have everlasting life.

1. So that means the Church is already in, or has already entered, the Kingdom of God?

1. Was it necessary for Jesus to die? Was there any other way to reconcile humanity back to God without Jesus dying on the cross?

1. Are there instances in the Bible where sin was forgiven without the shedding of blood?

1. What about David and Nineveh?

1. So that means the shedding of blood for forgiveness only worked under the Jewish law, or the Law of Moses, meaning it was only applicable to Jews at that time. Therefore, Jesus accomplished reconciliation and forgiveness according to the requirements of that law.

1. If God didn't destroy Nineveh because they repented—similar to the parable of the two servants regarding forgiveness—it might seem as though He did not practice justice by giving people what they deserve. However, Jesus said that we must show mercy as well as justice, doing good to both the good and the evil, just as the Father does.

   While God is merciful, withholding justice can make it seem as though He is unjust. Yet, we know He is just, and since He is the one to whom we owe our debt, it is entirely up to Him whether or not to forgive our sins. Because He is merciful and good, He gives us grace and forgives our sins when we repent, giving us a second chance to leave a life of sin and turn back to Him in obedience. I think God withholds His justice and shows His mercy because His grace abounds even more than sin.

   Ultimately, He is the one who acquits or condemns us. To remain fair and just, if someone comes to the Lord demanding justice and calling for a sinner to be condemned, God can always pay the ransom Himself or place someone—or even Himself—in the position of the sinner whom He loves. Is this correct?

